The following is taken from {{CRED-DELEG}} and was not checked against the code. The implementation follows the same division of custody: provider credentials stay in the server's vault, the server makes the upstream call, and by default the server hands no provider token to an agent. It differs from this document as follows. An agent holds a Delegation Token as defined in {{CRED-DELEG}}, and not an access token obtained with the client credentials grant, and the `brokered_access` type is not used. An exercise request names an upstream URL, method, and body at a single endpoint, where this document has the instance send an ordinary request to a broker resource. Requests are checked against a catalogue of operations for two providers.

# Open Issues {#open}

- Restricting a brokered authorization to some instances of the logical client, for example by attester, by tenant, or by a condition the resource owner sets ({{any-instance}}).
- An instance that starts a further instance of a different logical client. This document covers only instances of the client the brokered authorization was made to.
- Client instances that authenticate with an assertion issued by their platform and not with a key of their own.
- A broker as the enforcement point for authorization details types defined elsewhere, such as that of {{MISSION-RA}}.
- How a client learns the operation names a broker resource uses.
- The forwarding rules listed in {{forwarding}}, which are candidates for a companion document.
- Upstream resources that answer with a challenge for stronger authentication or for interactive consent.
- Evidence of each request sent upstream that a party other than the broker can check.
- Operation of the broker and its authorization server by separate parties.

